1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,350
Inside a 4,500-year-old pyramid, there is a space no one has ever entered.

2
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:08,300
It's the Great Pyramid of Khufu in Giza, Egypt.

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:14,200
To look inside without breaking a stone, they used 'muons', tiny particles from space.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:20,050
Rock blocks some muons, but more get through where there is empty space.

5
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:25,200
In 2017, the data showed a big empty space above the 'Grand Gallery'.

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:29,300
It's at least 30 m long. Some said it's as big as a passenger plane.

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:33,200
It's the first big structure found inside the pyramid since the 1800s.

8
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:37,300
But what it was for, no one knows yet.

9
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:42,950
The deepest hole people ever dug is only 23 cm wide.

10
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:47,000
It is the Kola Superdeep Borehole, in the far north of Russia.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,400
From 1970, for over 20 years, they drilled down into the Earth.

12
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:58,400
It reached 12,262 m. Even upside-down Everest would leave over 3 km to go.

13
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,350
But at the bottom, it got as hot as 180°C.

14
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:07,200
That's much hotter than the 100°C they expected. The rock got soft like plastic.

15
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:11,750
So they stopped drilling and welded a metal cap on top.

16
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:16,200
After all that, they didn't even reach 0.1% of the way through the Earth.

17
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:22,250
In 1963 in Türkiye, a man was fixing his house and knocked down a wall.

18
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:25,200
Behind that wall was a tunnel with no end in sight.

19
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:29,600
It was a huge underground city, going 85 m down.

20
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:33,050
People dug into soft volcanic rock and made rooms, floor by floor.

21
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:36,250
About 20,000 people could hide here, with their animals.

22
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:40,050
Air shafts go up to the surface and bring air deep down.

23
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:44,850
The doors were round stones of hundreds of kg, and could only be rolled from inside.

24
00:01:44,850 --> 00:01:48,400
When enemies came, the whole city disappeared underground.

25
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:53,350
Under the streets of New York, 500,000 gold bars are sleeping.

26
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:57,000
They are under the New York Federal Reserve Bank, in the middle of Manhattan.

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,350
The vault is 24 m underground, on rock 15 m below sea level.

28
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:07,000
Only hard rock is strong enough to hold this much heavy gold.

29
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,800
The door is a 90-ton steel cylinder that turns to open and close.

30
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:15,600
There are about 6,331 tons of gold inside.

31
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,450
But most of this gold doesn't belong to the U.S.

32
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:21,650
Other countries and their central banks keep their gold here.

33
00:02:22,667 --> 00:02:26,617
Deep under Finland, there is a door that must stay closed for 100,000 years.

34
00:02:26,617 --> 00:02:31,017
This is Onkalo, the world's first place to store used nuclear fuel forever.

35
00:02:31,017 --> 00:02:35,617
A spiral tunnel goes down about 430 m into hard granite rock.

36
00:02:35,617 --> 00:02:39,167
The nuclear fuel goes into thick copper cans that don't rust easily.

37
00:02:39,167 --> 00:02:44,367
The cans go into 8.4 m deep holes, then clay fills every gap.

38
00:02:44,367 --> 00:02:49,317
Then the tunnels are filled too, so it stays safe with no one taking care of it.

39
00:02:49,317 --> 00:02:53,467
100,000 years is over 8 times longer than humans have been farming.

40
00:02:53,467 --> 00:02:57,967
We lock away what our electricity left behind, deep in the ground, for the far future.

41
00:02:58,967 --> 00:03:04,367
1,000 m under a mountain in Japan, there is a giant water tank covered in gold balls.

42
00:03:04,367 --> 00:03:08,517
It's Super-Kamiokande, hidden deep in an old mine in Gifu.

43
00:03:08,517 --> 00:03:12,867
The tank holds 50,000 tons of very clean water.

44
00:03:12,867 --> 00:03:18,117
On the walls are over 10,000 light sensors, each 50 cm wide.

45
00:03:18,117 --> 00:03:22,817
It's waiting for neutrinos, 'ghost particles' from space.

46
00:03:22,817 --> 00:03:27,717
Neutrinos hit almost nothing. They can pass right through the whole Earth.

47
00:03:27,717 --> 00:03:32,117
Very rarely, one hits the water and makes a faint blue flash.

48
00:03:32,117 --> 00:03:36,467
To catch that one flash, they went this deep underground.

49
00:03:37,467 --> 00:03:41,767
In a town in the Australian desert, you can hardly see any houses.

50
00:03:41,767 --> 00:03:45,217
That's because people dug into the hills and live underground.

51
00:03:45,217 --> 00:03:48,567
Here, summer gets way over 40°C.

52
00:03:48,567 --> 00:03:52,417
But the underground homes stay in the low 20s °C all year.

53
00:03:52,417 --> 00:03:56,417
They're cool without AC, and warm in winter without heating.

54
00:03:56,417 --> 00:04:00,117
This was a mining town where people dug for opal gems.

55
00:04:00,117 --> 00:04:03,667
The miners' tunnels became homes.

56
00:04:03,667 --> 00:04:07,767
Now there's even an underground church, hotel, and bookstore.

57
00:04:08,767 --> 00:04:12,317
Have you ever seen white steam coming up from the streets of New York?

58
00:04:12,317 --> 00:04:16,067
Under Manhattan, there are pipes full of hot steam.

59
00:04:16,067 --> 00:04:20,817
They started in 1882. Now they are over 160 km long.

60
00:04:20,817 --> 00:04:24,267
Power plants make the steam and send it to 1,500 buildings.

61
00:04:24,267 --> 00:04:27,967
The steam heats buildings, and in summer it even runs air conditioners.

62
00:04:27,967 --> 00:04:31,717
Hospitals also use it to clean surgery tools.

63
00:04:31,717 --> 00:04:36,467
Most street steam comes from rain hitting the hot pipes.

64
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:40,217
New York is really a city with a giant boiler underground.

65
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:44,900
Why doesn't Tokyo flood, even in big typhoons?

66
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:47,400
The secret is hidden 50 m underground.

67
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:52,550
A giant tunnel takes in extra river water and sends it to another river.

68
00:04:52,550 --> 00:04:57,300
At the end, there is a huge underground tank, 177 m long.

69
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:01,800
59 pillars, each 18 m tall, hold up the ceiling.

70
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:03,950
Each pillar weighs 500 tons!

71
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:09,000
They are this heavy to keep the tank from floating up on groundwater.

72
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,550
People call it the 'underground temple'. It keeps Tokyo safe every day.

73
00:05:14,567 --> 00:05:19,517
Japan has a train tunnel that runs over 53 km under the sea.

74
00:05:19,517 --> 00:05:24,167
It's the Seikan Tunnel, under the Tsugaru Strait between Honshu and Hokkaido.

75
00:05:24,167 --> 00:05:28,167
About 23 km of it runs under the sea floor.

76
00:05:28,167 --> 00:05:33,567
The tracks are 240 m below sea level, and 100 m below the sea floor.

77
00:05:33,567 --> 00:05:37,767
Back when people crossed by boat, a ferry sank in a big typhoon.

78
00:05:37,767 --> 00:05:42,217
So they decided to dig under the sea. It took over 20 years to build.

79
00:05:42,217 --> 00:05:46,417
Even now, pumps never stop pumping out seawater that leaks through the rock.

80
00:05:46,417 --> 00:05:50,117
Trains pass deep under the sea like it's nothing.

81
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:55,700
This video you're watching may have come through a hose under the sea.

82
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:01,350
Over 99% of data between continents goes through undersea cables, not satellites.

83
00:06:01,350 --> 00:06:05,250
In the deep sea, the cable is only as thick as a garden hose.

84
00:06:05,250 --> 00:06:10,100
Cut it open, and there are a few glass fibers as thin as hair in the middle.

85
00:06:10,100 --> 00:06:14,850
Around them are layers of copper, steel wire, and plastic.

86
00:06:14,850 --> 00:06:18,800
Light travels inside these glass fibers and carries the data.

87
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:23,150
The biggest danger is not sharks. It's fishing nets and ship anchors.

88
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:27,850
A few thin lines under the sea connect the whole world every day.

89
00:06:28,833 --> 00:06:33,983
4 km under the ice in Antarctica, a giant lake is hiding, and it's not frozen.

90
00:06:33,983 --> 00:06:37,883
It's Lake Vostok, under Russia's Vostok Station.

91
00:06:37,883 --> 00:06:41,983
It's about 230 km long, as big as Lake Ontario.

92
00:06:41,983 --> 00:06:46,483
For millions of years, it was trapped with no sunlight or wind.

93
00:06:46,483 --> 00:06:49,183
So why doesn't it freeze?

94
00:06:49,183 --> 00:06:54,733
The heavy ice above adds pressure, and heat from the Earth warms it from below.

95
00:06:54,733 --> 00:07:01,133
In 2012, a Russian team drilled about 3,769 m and finally reached the lake.

96
00:07:01,133 --> 00:07:05,333
When they reached it, the water was pushed up and shot up the hole.

97
00:07:06,366 --> 00:07:11,816
There is a seed vault in an Arctic mountain, so we can open it even if the world ends.

98
00:07:11,816 --> 00:07:15,416
It's in Svalbard, Norway, a frozen island close to the North Pole.

99
00:07:15,416 --> 00:07:19,266
A tunnel goes about 130 m into the side of the mountain.

100
00:07:19,266 --> 00:07:23,416
At the end, there are three rooms full of seed boxes.

101
00:07:23,416 --> 00:07:26,316
Inside, it is always kept at -18°C.

102
00:07:26,316 --> 00:07:31,316
Even without power, the frozen ground keeps it around -3°C.

103
00:07:31,316 --> 00:07:37,066
Now more than 1.3 million seed samples from around the world sleep here.

104
00:07:37,066 --> 00:07:42,616
In 2015, a lab in war-torn Syria became the first to take seeds out.
